#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083039 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/id #uuid "cdd74ee4-b82f-520f-8541-f328f9e63fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8279" :question/problem "The birth weights of newborns in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $3\\,543$ grams and a standard deviation of $609$ grams. What is the probability that a randomly selected newborn baby weighs more than $3\\,100$ grams but less than $3\\,600$? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$$\\, 0.01 \\,$$\\, 0.02 \\,$$\\, 0.03 \\,$$\\, -0.8 \\,$ $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ $\\, 0.2033 \\,$ $\\, -0.7 \\,$ $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ $\\, 0.2327 \\,$ $\\, -0.6 \\,$ $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ $\\, 0.2643 \\,$ |  | $z$$\\, 0.08 \\,$$\\, 0.09 \\,$$\\, 0.10 \\,$$\\, -0.1 \\,$ $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ $\\, 0.4207 \\,$ $\\, \\phantom{-}0.0 \\,$ $\\, 0.5319 \\,$ $\\, 0.5359 \\,$ $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ $\\, \\phantom{-}0.1 \\,$ $\\, 0.5714 \\,$ $\\, 0.5753 \\,$ $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the weight of a randomly selected baby. Then we have $X \\sim N(3\\,543, 609^2)$.\n\nWe want to find $P(3\\,100 < X < 3\\,600)$. First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(3100 < X < 3600) &= P(\\frac{3100 - 3543}{609} < Z < \\frac{3600 - 3543}{609}) \\\\\n&= P(-0.73 < Z < 0.09)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.73 < Z < 0.09) &= P(Z < 0.09) - P(Z < - 0.73) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 0.09) - P(Z \\le - 0.73) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.09) - Φ(-0.73)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ(-0.73) &= 0.2327 \\\\\nΦ(0.09) &= 0.5359\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.73 < Z < 0.09) &= Φ(0.09) - Φ(-0.73) \\\\\n&= 0.5359 - 0.2327 \\\\\n&= 0.3032\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the probability that a randomly selected newborn baby weighs more than $3\\,100$ grams but less than $3\\,600$ is $0.30$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/id #uuid "5905ffa9-e7e2-5288-8714-91357fb17139" :question/math-academy-id "e-8280" :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the episodes of a popular podcast is $43\\,\\textrm{min}$. Additionally, $20\\%$ of the episodes have a duration of less than $38$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of a randomly selected episode is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the episode durations, rounded to the nearest minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ | $\\, 0.15 \\,$ | $\\, 0.20 \\,$ | $\\, 0.25 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, -2.3263 \\,$ | $\\, -1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, -1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, -1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, -1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, -0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, -0.6745 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly selected episode. Then, we have $X \\sim N(43, \\sigma^2)$, and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma$.\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq 38) = 0.20\n$$\n\nConverting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 38) &= 0.20 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{38 - 43}{σ}) &= 0.20 \\\\\nΦ(-\\frac{5}{σ}) &= 0.20 \\\\\n- \\frac{5}{σ} &= Φ^{-1}(0.20)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table,\n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.20) =-0.8416\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{5}{σ} &= -0.8416 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{σ} &= 0.1683 \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{1}{0.1683} \\\\\n&= σ \\mid \\approx 6minutes\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "4049eb36-a8b0-5ef7-bf82-6f38cbe5cb17" :question/math-academy-id "e-8281" :question/problem "The systolic blood pressure of a group of people is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $12\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$. Additionally, $50\\%$ of the people have a systolic blood pressure between $0.93\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and $1.07\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$. Find, as a percentage, the proportion of people with a systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.75 \\,$ | $\\, 0.80 \\,$ | $\\, 0.85 \\,$ | $\\, 0.90 \\,$ | $\\, 0.95 \\,$ | $\\, 0.97 \\,$ | $\\, 0.99 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, 0.6745 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, 1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, 1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, 1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, 1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, 2.3263 \\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p = \\Phi(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6026 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6406 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the systolic blood pressure of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X \\sim N(\\mu, 12^2)$, and we want to find $P(X < 120)$. But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu$.\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nP(0.93\\mu < X < 1.07\\mu) = 0.5\n$$\n\nFirst, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0.93μ < X < 1.07μ) &= 0.5 \\\\\n2P(μ < X < 1.07μ) &= 0.5 \\\\\nP(μ < X < 1.07μ) &= 0.25 \\\\\nP(X < μ) + P(μ < X < 1.07μ) &= 0.5 + 0.25 \\\\\nP(X < 1.07μ) &= 0.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 1.07μ) &= 0.75 \\\\\nP(Z < \\frac{0.07μ}{12}) &= 0.75 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{0.07μ}{12}) &= 0.75 \\\\\n\\frac{0.07μ}{12} &= Φ^{-1}(0.75)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table,\n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.75) \\approx 0.6745\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{0.07μ}{12} &= 0.6745 \\\\\n0.07μ &= 8.094 \\\\\nμ &= \\frac{8.094}{0.07} \\\\\n&\\approx 115.629\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $P(X < 120)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 120) &= P(Z < \\frac{120 - 115.629}{12}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(Z < 0.36) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.36) \\\\\n&= 0.6406 \\\\\n&\\approx 64.1%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $64.1\\%$ of the people have systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083058 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"}] :question/id #uuid "c55db5a3-2d8c-57d7-b860-8628cb8738f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9892" :question/problem "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/cdec8b9ece66152d62f2dbb412e1ffbd.png)" :question/worked-solution "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped.\n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is shown in diagram II:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/aaad3df612dabb3df1f8277d8a90f5d0.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fa31037-44ef-5261-8775-57dea5d4b13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079977} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2db0d3-e7ef-5209-ae1a-a5840baaf6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079978} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ddb6ca-634f-50bb-be7d-4f70f15b69c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079979} {:answer/id #uuid "011a9a4e-038d-540d-b262-c8358f98da89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079980} {:answer/id #uuid "76b5ed90-5a35-5bc0-9f5f-d7e6dcb9fc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/788/Source/Images/q-139110-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b5ed90-5a35-5bc0-9f5f-d7e6dcb9fc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/h